@block introduction | start | 1.1 | Every cosine solution in one turn
@prose This reading solves exact cosine equations for real angles theta measured in radians on 0<=theta<2pi. A complete answer contains every permitted angle once.
@endblock
@block definition | terms | 1.2 | Radians, angles, and horizontal coordinate
@prose A radian measures an angle by arc length on a unit circle, a circle of radius 1 centered at the origin. Pi is the exact angle of a half-turn and 2pi is one full turn. Theta is a real radian angle; alpha names one known solution angle, which need not be acute. On the unit circle, cos(theta) is the horizontal coordinate. A solution set contains every theta that makes the original equation true.
@prose Sine, abbreviated sin, is the vertical coordinate; cosine, abbreviated cos, is the horizontal coordinate. Writing sin theta or cos theta without parentheses means the same function value as sin(theta) or cos(theta).
@display coordinate
\cos(\theta)=\text{horizontal coordinate on the unit circle}
@prose Zero is included and 2pi is excluded, so their shared direction is counted once. An integer parameter k records repeated full turns.
@prose Angles start on the positive horizontal axis and increase counterclockwise. The four quadrants, in that order, have coordinate signs (+,+), (-,+), (-,-), (+,-). Here x means horizontal coordinate and y means vertical coordinate. Braces list a set without order or repetition; the empty-set symbol means no members. Square brackets include an endpoint and round brackets exclude it. The symbol sqrt(3) denotes the positive number whose square is 3.
@endblock
@block proposition | rule | 1.3 | Locate, repeat, and filter
@prose Isolate cosine, locate every unit-circle point with that horizontal coordinate, add 2kpi for integer k, and filter the interval. Reflection across the horizontal axis maps alpha to 2pi-alpha and preserves horizontal coordinate. This is not the sine reflection.
@display reflection
\cos(2\pi-\alpha)=\cos(\alpha)
@help proof
@prose A vertical line at a permitted horizontal coordinate meets the unit circle at the listed one-turn points: two for a non-extreme coordinate and one at coordinate 1 or -1. Adding full turns and filtering [0,2pi) gives all permitted angles exactly once.
@prose For completeness, the unit-circle equation is x squared+y squared=1. Fixing either coordinate at h leaves the other coordinate squared equal to 1-h squared: two roots when -1<h<1, one when h is 1 or -1, and none when the magnitude of h exceeds 1. Membership and this count together prove a complete set; remembering one angle alone does not.
@prose Bisect an equilateral triangle of side 2. Its right triangle has hypotenuse 2, short leg 1 and long leg sqrt(3), because 2 squared-1 squared=3. Its small angle is pi/6 and its other acute angle is pi/3. Dividing the lengths by 2 gives sine(pi/6)=1/2 and cosine(pi/3)=1/2. Reflect these points using the quadrant signs for negative halves; the axis points supply zero and the extreme coordinates. Add or subtract 2pi until an angle is in the stated turn. At an extreme the reflected point coincides with the original and must not be counted twice.
@endblock
@block example | condition | 1.4 | Isolate without changing solutions
@prose In A cos(theta)+B=C, A is the nonzero integer coefficient multiplying cosine, B is the added constant, and C is the right-side constant. Subtract B from both complete sides and divide by nonzero A. The inverse operations recover the original equation; division by zero is undefined.
@display isolate
A\cos(\theta)+B=C\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\cos(\theta)=\frac{C-B}{A}
@endblock
@block example | worked | 1.5 | A complete worked extreme-coordinate example
@prose Solve the following equation in the stated interval.
@display worked_given
2\cos(\theta)+3=5,\qquad0\le\theta<2\pi
@help hint
@prose Subtract 3 from both complete sides, divide by 2, then locate the horizontal coordinate.
@help answer
@display
\theta\in\left\{0\right\}
@help solution
@prose Subtracting 3 gives 2cos(theta)=5-3=2. Dividing by 2 gives cos(theta)=1. Horizontal coordinate 1 occurs only at the rightmost unit-circle point, theta=0. The check is 2cos(0)+3=2(1)+3=5. The periodic copy 2pi is excluded, so only zero remains in [0,2pi).
@display worked_check
2\cos(0)+3=5
@endblock
@block example | errors | 1.6 | Locate the first endpoint error
@prose Check branch validity before a substituted list. An added 2pi violates the strict upper endpoint, even though it points in the same direction as zero.
@endblock
@block exercise | practice | 1.7 | Distinguish a coordinate from a complete set
@prose The exercises distinguish a cosine value, an angle, a horizontal-axis reflection, and a complete solution set. The fresh question provides no prescribed intermediate method.
@endblock
@block summary | summary | 1.8 | Membership plus completeness
@prose Isolate cosine, list every coordinate-matching point, repeat by 2kpi, filter [0,2pi), and check each retained angle. The vertical-line intersection argument establishes completeness. Other coordinate values may require additional reference angles or inverse functions.
@endblock
